/**
 * @module zuri
 *
 * Zuri's own compiler pipeline, as a library: lexing, parsing and
 * compiling Zuri source, and a `reflect` submodule for inspecting live
 * functions, classes and modules at run time.
 *
 * - `tokenize(source)` returns every token in `source`, in order. See
 *   {zuri.token}.
 * - `parse(source)` returns the syntax tree of `source` as a list of
 *   nodes, with every comment and doc block kept in place. See
 *   {zuri.ast}.
 * - `compile(source)` returns the VM instructions that `source`
 *   compiles to. See {zuri.compile}.
 * - `parse_partial(source)` reads as much of `source` as it can, and
 *   returns the tree along with every syntax error, and `check(source)`
 *   returns every problem that compiling `source` would report. Both
 *   return {zuri.Diagnostic} objects, and neither raises an error for
 *   invalid source. See {zuri.ast} and {zuri.compile}.
 * - `zuri.doc` reads documentation comments: the text and `@tag`s of a
 *   doc block, and which declaration each block documents. See
 *   {zuri.doc}.
 * - `zuri.reflect` inspects live functions, classes and modules, reads
 *   and updates an instance's own properties, and runs the garbage
 *   collector on demand. See {zuri.reflect}.
 * - `zuri.watch` tells `zuri run --watch` about files that a program
 *   depends on besides its own source, so that a change to one of them
 *   restarts the program. See {zuri.watch}.
 *
 * In practice, you usually start with a file on disk, not a string of
 * source, so each of these also has a `_file` version that reads `path`
 * first: `tokenize_file(path)`, `parse_file(path)`,
 * `compile_file(path)`, `parse_partial_file(path)` and
 * `check_file(path)`. There's also `dump_file(path)`, which has no
 * version that takes source. It reads and parses `path`, and returns
 * the `dump()` of every top-level node, which is usually the quickest
 * way to find out what's in a file.
 *
 * ## How it fits together
 *
 * `tokenize()`, `parse()` and `compile()` all return the same kind of
 * thing: a list of small records, each tagged in the same way. A token
 * has a `kind`, a syntax tree node has a `kind`, and an instruction has
 * an `op`. Whichever one you have, everything specific to that kind is
 * in one place: `token.value`, `node.fields` or `instr.fields`. There's
 * one `Token` class, one `Node` class and one `Instr` class, not a
 * class for every kind of token, grammar rule or opcode. Zuri's grammar
 * has around fifty kinds of expression, statement and declaration, and
 * the instruction set has around sixty opcodes. A class for each would
 * mean well over a hundred almost identical classes to keep in step
 * with the compiler, each one only a name and a few fields. Python's
 * `ast` module and Babel's ESTree make the same trade-off, for the same
 * reason.
 *
 * The cost of that choice is that nothing tells you which fields a
 * particular `kind` or `op` has, as a typed class would. You can't use
 * autocompletion to find a `Binary` node's `left`, `op` and `right`. So
 * this module relies on a different way of finding out: run something
 * and look at it, instead of reading a specification first.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }')
 * echo nodes[0].dump()
 * ```
 * ```
 * Function@1:1
 *   name: 'add'
 *   name_span: 1:5-1:8
 *   parameters:
 *     Argument@1:9
 *       name: 'a'
 *       name_span: 1:9-1:10
 *       type_hint:
 *         TypeHint@1:10
 *           types:
 *             Any@1:10
 *               class_name: nil
 *           nullable: true
 *     ...
 *   body:
 *     Block@1:15
 *       statements:
 *         Return@1:17
 *           value:
 *             Binary@1:24
 *               left:
 *                 Identifier@1:24
 *                   name: 'a'
 *               op: 'Plus'
 *               right:
 *                 Identifier@1:28
 *                   name: 'b'
 *   is_variadic: false
 * ```
 *
 * That one `dump()` call shows what a `Function` node looks like far
 * more completely than a paragraph of text could, and it works for
 * whatever kind of node you actually have, not just the ones someone
 * thought to describe. {zuri.ast.Node.dump} and
 * {zuri.compile.Instr.dump} both work this way. {zuri.token.Token}
 * doesn't need it, because a token always has the same seven fields,
 * `kind`, `line`, `column`, `start`, `end`, `text` and `value`, which
 * are described in the documentation of {zuri.token}.
 *
 * Once you know the `kind` or `op` you're interested in, `walk_nodes()`
 * and `walk_instrs()`, or their shorthand forms, `find_nodes()` and
 * `find_instrs()`, do the actual work. Almost nothing useful is done by
 * indexing into `fields` one node at a time. A typical task is "for
 * every node or instruction of kind X, do Y", however deep in the tree
 * it is, whether inside a nested function, inside an `if` or anywhere
 * else, and that's what these functions are for, so use them first.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 *
 * # The name of every function declared in the file, including nested
 * # functions.
 * var names = []
 * zuri.walk_nodes(nodes, {
 *   Function: @(node) { names.append(node.fields.name) },
 * })
 *
 * # The shorthand, for when you only want to collect them.
 * var comments = zuri.find_nodes(nodes, 'Comment')
 * ```
 *
 * So the usual workflow is:
 *
 * 1. Call `parse_file()` or `compile_file()` on a real file, or
 *    `parse()` or `compile()` if you already have the source as a
 *    string. If you just want to look before writing any code against
 *    it, run `echo zuri.dump_file(path)` first.
 * 2. Call `dump()` on the node or instruction you're interested in, to
 *    see its actual shape.
 * 3. Use `walk_nodes()` or `walk_instrs()`, or `find_nodes()` or
 *    `find_instrs()`, to do something with every node or instruction of
 *    that kind, wherever it appears.
 * 4. Only read the documentation of {zuri.ast} or {zuri.compile} for
 *    what exploring doesn't tell you, such as what a node's position
 *    covers, or how a `Closure` instruction's resolved value contains a
 *    whole nested list of instructions.
 *
 * `zuri.reflect` doesn't need any of this. It's a set of ordinary named
 * functions, such as `function_info(f)` and `has_method(object, name)`,
 * with ordinary parameters, not a system of tagged records, so its own
 * documentation tells you everything. See {zuri.reflect}.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * for t in zuri.tokenize('var x = 1') {
 *   echo t.kind
 * }
 *
 * for node in zuri.parse('var x = 1') {
 *   echo node.kind
 * }
 *
 * for instr in zuri.compile('var x = 1') {
 *   echo instr.op
 * }
 *
 * def add(a, b) {
 *   return a + b
 * }
 * echo zuri.reflect.function_info(add).arity
 * ```
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import @.token { * }
import @.ast { * }
import @.compile { * }
import @.diagnostic { * }
import @.doc
import @.reflect
import @.watch
